==English==

===Etymology 1===
From ''{{w|Gulliver's Travels}}'' by {{w|Jonathan Swift}}, where {{m|en|Yahoo}} is the name of a race of [[brute]]s.<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈjɑːhuː/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-yahoo.oga|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑːhu|s=2}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|pejorative}} A [[rough]], [[coarse]], [[loud]] or [[uncouth]] [[individual]].
#* {{quote-av|1=en|date=21 April 2012|actor={{w|Janet Varney}}|role={{w|Korra}}|director={{w|Joaquim Dos Santos}}; Ki Hyun Ryu|writer={{w|Michael Dante DiMartino}}; {{w|Bryan Konietzko}}|title={{w|The Legend of Korra}}|season=1|number=3|episode=The Revelation|time=7:01|text=Triple Threat Triad? I beat up some of those '''yahoos''' when I got into town.}}
# {{lb|en|cryptozoology}} A [[humanoid]] [[cryptid]] said to [[exist]] in [[part]]s of [[eastern]] [[Australia]], and also [[reported]] in the [[Bahamas]].
#* '''1835''', James Holman, ''Travels'', quoted by Malcolm Smith, ''Bunyips and Bigfoots'' (Millennium Books, 1996, {{ISBN|1-86429-081-1}}), who notes that the Australian sense almost certainly derives from ''Gulliver's Travels'', despite Holman's report
#*: ''The natives are greatly terrrified by the sight of a person in a mask calling him "devil" or Yah-hoo, which signifies evil spirit.''
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1985|author=Michael Raynal|title='''Yahoos''' in the Bahamas: Cryptozoology|volume=4}}

